{
  "query": "An online learning startup named BrightLearn serves most of its users in France, and there are no legal data residency requirements to keep information in France or within the European Union. As the Cloud Database Engineer you have chosen Cloud SQL and must decide where to provision the database instance to achieve the best responsiveness for your users. What should you do?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in any Google Cloud region anywhere in the world",
      "explanation": "This option means you select a region without considering the geographic location of your users or your application workloads",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in a European dual-region to balance performance across the continent",
      "explanation": "This option places data in two European locations to emphasize durability and availability across multiple sites",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in a Google Cloud region that is located in France",
      "explanation": "This option places the database close to the majority of users which commonly minimizes network latency and improves perceived performance",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the instance in us-east1 to take advantage of a large number of zones and capacity",
      "explanation": "This option prioritizes a large and mature US region that can support resilient deployments and scaling needs",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Create the instance in a Google Cloud region that is located in France</b>.</p><p>User responsiveness depends heavily on network latency, and placing the database near where most users are located keeps round trips short. There are no legal residency constraints in this scenario, so selecting a nearby regional location is the most effective way to improve performance for connections from clients and application servers. Cloud SQL is a regional service, and proximity to users is the simplest and most reliable way to reduce latency and variability for reads and writes.</p><p><i>Create the instance in any Google Cloud region anywhere in the world</i> is not appropriate because region selection strongly influences latency and throughput. A distant region can add significant network delay to each query which harms user experience.</p><p><i>Create the instance in a European dual-region to balance performance across the continent</i> is unnecessary for a user base concentrated in one country and does not match how Cloud SQL instances are provisioned. Cloud SQL does not provide a dual region primary instance, and introducing cross region replicas adds cost and complexity without solving the core latency need.</p><p><i>Create the instance in us-east1 to take advantage of a large number of zones and capacity</i> is not suitable because additional zones do not offset the transatlantic latency for users in France. Capacity considerations do not outweigh proximity in this case and the distance would degrade responsiveness.</p>",
